index 4479b53..284df47 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -15,3 +15,77 @@ The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Account settings and passkey service
+
+Account settings live at `/devtools-times/account` and are linked from the header.
+Sign-in now goes through a same-origin endpoint, which stores the service bearer
+token in Astro's server-side session, rotates the session ID, and sends only an
+HTTP-only session cookie to the browser. Sign-out destroys the session. The Node
+adapter's default session store is filesystem based; deployments with multiple
+instances need a shared Astro session driver.
+
+The existing remote login service does **not** currently expose `/passkeys`
+(the endpoint returns 404). This repository does not contain that service's
+credential database or registration implementation. Management therefore requires
+an upstream implementation of the following contract; the UI shows a service error
+rather than inventing registered passkeys. Set the server-only `TIMES_API_URL`
+environment variable at build time to use another compatible API base URL.
+
+- `POST /login`: accepts `{ "username": "…", "password": "…" }`; returns
+  `{ "token": "…" }` on successful authentication.
+- `GET /passkeys`: returns the signed-in reader's **complete**, unpaginated snapshot below.
+- `PATCH /passkeys/:id`: accepts `{ "displayName": "Personal laptop" }` and returns
+  the complete snapshot after persisting the rename.
+- `DELETE /passkeys/:id`: revokes the credential for authentication and returns
+  the complete snapshot after deletion, including an empty list when appropriate.
+
+All passkey endpoints receive `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The upstream must
+validate the session and credential ownership on every request. Return `401` for
+expired sessions, `403` for forbidden changes, and `409` if removal would leave
+the account without an available sign-in method. Per-key display names must be
+trimmed and between 1 and 64 characters. The mutation responses must contain the
+snapshot, rather than a `204` or a single updated key.
+
+```json
+{
+  "complete": true,
+  "rpId": "your-registered-rp.example",
+  "user": {
+    "id": "dXNlci1oYW5kbGU",
+    "name": "reader@example.com",
+    "displayName": "Reader"
+  },
+  "passkeys": [
+    {
+      "id": "Y3JlZGVudGlhbC1pZA",
+      "displayName": "Personal laptop",
+      "createdAt": "2026-10-01T12:00:00Z",
+      "lastUsedAt": null
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+`rpId` must be the actual RP ID used at registration and valid for the site's
+origin. `user.id` is the original WebAuthn user handle, encoded as base64url;
+credential IDs use base64url too. Dates are ISO timestamps; `lastUsedAt` can be
+`null`. The `complete: true` marker explicitly guarantees that no accepted key is
+omitted, since signaling a partial list could hide valid credentials.
+
+After a successful load or mutation, supported secure browsers receive
+`signalAllAcceptedCredentials` with the complete server list, followed by
+`signalCurrentUserDetails` with the account's identity. Per-key labels stay local
+to the site's registry: the Signal API updates account identity, not individual
+key nicknames. Signals are best effort and provider controlled; unsupported or
+rejected signals leave the account change intact and display manual cleanup
+guidance. See the [WebAuthn Signal API explainer](https://github.com/w3c/webauthn/blob/main/explainers/signal-api.md).
+
+Run the isolated account and synchronization tests with Node 24+:
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types --test tests/passkeys.test.ts
+```
+
+These tests mock the upstream service and browser signal methods; they do not
+start a server, import the built server entrypoint, or exercise a live authenticator.
